<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
<title>LLMs for Fault Localization — Empirical Study</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="static/style.css" />
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>LLMs for Fault Localization</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      An empirical comparison of LLMs at pointing out buggy lines in Python
      methods, with and without test outcomes and spectrum-based ranking.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      Topics in Software Engineering — Assignment 2 &middot;
      Built {{ built_at }} &middot;
      Commit <code>{{ env.git_commit[:7] }}</code>
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<nav class="toc">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
    <a href="#dataset">Dataset</a>
    <a href="#design">Design</a>
    <a href="#validation">Infra Validation</a>
    <a href="#results">Results</a>
    <a href="#qualitative">Qualitative</a>
    <a href="#threats">Threats</a>
    <a href="#reproducibility">Reproducibility</a>
    <a href="#ai-tools">AI Tools</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<main class="wrap">

<section id="overview">
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    This study measures how well large language models identify the faulty
    line in a small buggy Python method, and whether extra hints —
    passing/failing tests and a Tarantula-based suspicion ranking — actually
    help. We compare <strong>{{ n_models }} models</strong> across
    <strong>4 information conditions</strong> on a hand-mutated benchmark
    of <strong>{{ n_tasks }} HumanEval methods</strong>
    ({{ n_tasks }} × {{ n_models }} × 4 = {{ n_tasks * n_models * 4 }}
    total LLM calls).
  </p>

  <h3>Research questions</h3>
  <dl>
    <dt>RQ1 — Baseline</dt>
    <dd>How well do LLMs localize faults with only the buggy method and its spec?</dd>
    <dt>RQ2 — Effect of tests</dt>
    <dd>Do passing/failing test outcomes improve LLM fault-localization?</dd>
    <dt>RQ3 — Effect of SBFL</dt>
    <dd>Does providing a Tarantula-style ranking improve performance?</dd>
    <dt>RQ4 — Combining</dt>
    <dd>Does tests + SBFL help more than either alone?</dd>
    <dt>RQ5 — Model comparison</dt>
    <dd>Do different LLMs perform differently on the same task?</dd>
  </dl>

  {% if results.overall %}
  <h3>Headline numbers</h3>
  <table class="summary">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Overall</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Top-1 accuracy</td><td>{{ (results.overall.top1_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Top-3 accuracy</td><td>{{ (results.overall.top3_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Region accuracy</td><td>{{ (results.overall.region_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mean reciprocal rank</td><td>{{ results.overall.mrr | round(3) }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Invalid-output rate</td><td>{{ (results.overall.invalid_rate * 100) | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% else %}
  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Note:</strong> real experiment results not yet available.
    Numbers appear here once <code>scripts/run_llm.py</code> has been run
    against OpenRouter and <code>scripts/compute_metrics.py</code> has
    aggregated them.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="dataset">
  <h2>Dataset</h2>
  <p>
    The benchmark contains {{ n_tasks }} short Python methods based on
    HumanEval. Each method has:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a natural-language specification (docstring from HumanEval)</li>
    <li>an <code>original.py</code> that passes all tests</li>
    <li>a <code>buggy.py</code> with exactly one mutation applied</li>
    <li>at least 10 pytest tests (many from HumanEval, plus hand-written extras that exercise the mutated line)</li>
    <li>a <code>meta.json</code> with the ground-truth faulty line and mutation type</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mutation types (spread across {{ mutation_counts | length }} categories)</h3>
  <table class="counts">
    <thead><tr><th>Mutation type</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for kind, count in mutation_counts.items() %}
      <tr><td>{{ kind }}</td><td>{{ count }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>All {{ n_tasks }} benchmark tasks</h3>
  <table class="dataset">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Task</th>
        <th>Function</th>
        <th>Mutation type</th>
        <th>Region</th>
        <th>Line(s)</th>
        <th>Tests</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in dataset %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ row.task_id }}</code></td>
        <td><code>{{ row.entry_point }}</code></td>
        <td>{{ row.mutation_type }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.faulty_region }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.faulty_lines | join(", ") }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.n_tests }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Example mutations</h3>
  {% for row in dataset[:3] %}
  <div class="example">
    <p><strong>{{ row.task_id }}</strong> — {{ row.mutation_description }}</p>
    <pre>{{ row.buggy_snippet }}</pre>
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="design">
  <h2>Experimental Design</h2>

  <h3>Models</h3>
  <ul>
    {% for m in models %}<li><code>{{ m }}</code></li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p>Decoding: <code>temperature = {{ temperature }}</code>, one call per prompt.</p>

  <h3>Information conditions</h3>
  <table class="conditions">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Buggy code</th><th>Spec</th><th>Tests</th><th>Tarantula ranking</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A — code only</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>B — + tests</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>C — + SBFL</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&#x2714;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>D — both</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td><td>&#x2714;</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Metrics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Top-1 accuracy</strong> — predicted top line equals a ground-truth faulty line</li>
    <li><strong>Top-3 accuracy</strong> — any of the predicted top 3 lines matches</li>
    <li><strong>Region accuracy</strong> — Top-1 line matches, OR the predicted region label mentions the ground-truth region</li>
    <li><strong>Mean reciprocal rank (MRR)</strong> — 1/rank of the first correct line in top-3, else 0</li>
    <li><strong>Invalid-output rate</strong> — fraction of responses missing the required JSON top_1_line</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Prompt templates (one per condition)</h3>
  <p>Each prompt places the buggy code with 1-indexed line prefixes and
  asks the model to return JSON with <code>top_1_line</code>,
  <code>top_3_lines</code>, <code>faulty_region</code>, and
  <code>explanation</code>. Templates live in <code>prompts/</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="validation">
  <h2>Validation of the Experiment Infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    A key part of this assignment is that the pipeline is itself software
    we're expected to test. Our infrastructure test suite lives under
    <code>tests/</code>:
  </p>
  <table class="summary">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Number of infrastructure test functions</td><td>{{ infra.n_tests }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Number of test files</td><td>{{ infra.n_test_files }}</td></tr>
      {% if infra.coverage_percent is not none %}
      <tr><td>Overall line+branch coverage on <code>src/</code></td>
          <td>{{ infra.coverage_percent | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
      {% endif %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Tests exercise:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tarantula and Ochiai suspicion formulas (edge cases + tie-breaking)</li>
    <li>LLM response parser (fenced JSON, prose slop, invalid inputs)</li>
    <li>Scoring metrics (Top-1/3, MRR, region hit, invalid rate)</li>
    <li>Mutation helpers</li>
    <li>Prompt template rendering per condition</li>
    <li>Coverage runner helpers (context normalization, JUnit parsing)</li>
    <li>SBFL aggregator (spectra construction, report shape)</li>
    <li>End-to-end: real pytest run against HE_000 with per-test coverage</li>
    <li>Condition-gating in <code>run_llm.py</code></li>
    <li>End-to-end: metrics pipeline against a synthetic-response fixture</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="results">
  <h2>Results</h2>
  {% if results.overall %}
    <p>
      Aggregated across <strong>{{ results.n_calls }}</strong> calls
      ({{ n_tasks }} tasks × {{ n_models }} models × 4 conditions).
    </p>

    <h3>By information condition</h3>
    <table class="results">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Condition</th><th>N</th><th>Top-1</th><th>Top-3</th><th>Region</th><th>MRR</th><th>Invalid</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for cond, s in results.by_condition.items() %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ cond }}</td>
          <td>{{ s.n }}</td>
          <td>{{ (s.top1_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ (s.top3_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ (s.region_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ s.mrr | round(3) }}</td>
          <td>{{ (s.invalid_rate * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>By model</h3>
    <table class="results">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>N</th><th>Top-1</th><th>Top-3</th><th>Region</th><th>MRR</th><th>Invalid</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for m, s in results.by_model.items() %}
        <tr>
          <td><code>{{ m }}</code></td>
          <td>{{ s.n }}</td>
          <td>{{ (s.top1_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ (s.top3_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ (s.region_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ s.mrr | round(3) }}</td>
          <td>{{ (s.invalid_rate * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Per model × condition</h3>
    <table class="results">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Condition</th><th>Top-1</th><th>Top-3</th><th>MRR</th><th>Invalid</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for m, per_cond in results.by_model_condition.items() %}
          {% for cond, s in per_cond.items() %}
          <tr>
            <td><code>{{ m }}</code></td>
            <td>{{ cond }}</td>
            <td>{{ (s.top1_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
            <td>{{ (s.top3_accuracy * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
            <td>{{ s.mrr | round(3) }}</td>
            <td>{{ (s.invalid_rate * 100) | round(1) }}%</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    {% if has_plots %}
    <h3>Plots</h3>
    <div class="plots">
      <figure><img src="plots/top1_by_condition.png" alt="Top-1 by condition" /><figcaption>Top-1 accuracy by condition (per model)</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="plots/mrr_by_condition.png" alt="MRR by condition" /><figcaption>Mean reciprocal rank by condition</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="plots/invalid_by_condition.png" alt="Invalid rate by condition" /><figcaption>Invalid-output rate by condition</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  {% else %}
    <p class="callout">
      Results appear here once <code>scripts/run_llm.py</code> +
      <code>scripts/compute_metrics.py</code> have been run against
      OpenRouter. In the meantime, the same layout can be exercised
      against synthetic-response data via
      <code>python -m scripts.seed_synthetic_responses</code>.
    </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="qualitative">
  <h2>Qualitative Analysis</h2>
  {% if qualitative %}
  <p>Selected model explanations from the parsed responses:</p>
  {% for ex in qualitative %}
  <div class="example">
    <p><strong>{{ ex.task_id }}</strong> — model <code>{{ ex.model }}</code>, condition {{ ex.condition }}</p>
    <p>Predicted top-1: line {{ ex.top_1_line }}; top-3: {{ ex.top_3_lines | join(", ") }}</p>
    <blockquote>{{ ex.explanation }}</blockquote>
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
  {% else %}
  <p class="callout">
    Qualitative examples appear here once we have real LLM outputs to
    cherry-pick from. Planned categories: (a) tests helped catch a bug
    where code-only failed, (b) Tarantula pointed the model at the true
    line, (c) Tarantula misled the model, (d) plausible-but-wrong
    explanations.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="threats">
  <h2>Threats to Validity</h2>
  <pre class="doc">{{ docs.threats }}</pre>
</section>

<section id="reproducibility">
  <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
  <p>Full commands are documented in <code>README.md</code>. Short version:</p>
  <pre>
# 1. Setup
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1        # Windows
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env              # add OPENROUTER_API_KEY

# 2. Regenerate the dataset (verifies invariants)
mkdir -p .cache
curl -sL https://github.com/openai/human-eval/raw/master/data/HumanEval.jsonl.gz \
  -o .cache/HumanEval.jsonl.gz && gzip -df .cache/HumanEval.jsonl.gz
python -m scripts.scaffold_tasks

# 3. Pipeline
python -m scripts.run_sbfl        # per-task Tarantula rankings
python -m scripts.run_llm         # 240 LLM calls (needs API key)
python -m scripts.compute_metrics # aggregate metrics
python -m scripts.build_site      # rebuild this site
</pre>
  <table class="summary">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Python</td><td>{{ env.python_version }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Platform</td><td>{{ env.platform }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Git commit</td><td><code>{{ env.git_commit }}</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section id="ai-tools">
  <h2>Use of AI Tools</h2>
  <pre class="doc">{{ docs.ai_tools }}</pre>
</section>

</main>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Generated by <code>scripts/build_site.py</code>.</p>
  </div>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
